    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-2587",
      "problem": "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ.$ Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14173512/assets/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1=m∠2, \\\\ m∠3=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}=m∠2, \\\\ {120}^{∘}=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠4 & ={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
